# Philosophy of Mind: What Is Consciousness?

> You know what it is like to taste coffee; physics lists only
> masses, charges, and fields. This lesson examines the mind–body
> problem: how mental life relates to the brain — and what AI now
> forces us to decide about minds like and unlike our own.

## 1. The Mind–Body Problem

Mental states feel private, unified, and qualitative; brains are
public, divisible, and measurable. That these are somehow *the same
thing* is the puzzle philosophy of mind exists to solve — or
dissolve.

| Fact about experience | Fact about the brain |
| --- | --- |
| Felt from the inside | Observed from the outside |
| One unified point of view | Billions of separable neurons |
| Irreducibly qualitative ("red looks like *this*") | Described only in physical vocabulary |

@diagram tree id="mind-body-positions" layout="tree" aria="Main positions on the mind-body problem grouped into dualist, physicalist, and functionalist families"
  "Mind-body positions" -> "Dualism"
  "Mind-body positions" -> "Physicalism"
  "Mind-body positions" -> "Behaviourism"
  "Mind-body positions" -> "Functionalism"
  "Dualism" -> "Substance"
  "Dualism" -> "Property"
  "Physicalism" -> "Reductive"
  "Physicalism" -> "Non-reductive"
@/diagram

[definition]
**Consciousness**, in the philosophical sense, is *phenomenal*
consciousness: the fact that there is something it is like to be you
— Thomas Nagel's phrase, from "What Is It Like to Be a Bat?" (1974).
A bat echolocating has experiences; a thermostat, on most views,
does not — the line between them is the battlefield.
[/definition]

## 2. Substance Dualism: Descartes' Split

René Descartes (1596–1650) argued that mind and body are **distinct
substances**: the body is extended matter, the mind is thinking
stuff, known with certainty (*cogito, ergo sum*). Interaction, he
claimed, runs through the pineal gland.

| Strength | Problem |
| --- | --- |
| Honours the first-person certainty of experience | Causation: how does an unextended mind move matter? |
| Preserves free will and the soul | Violates the physical world's causal closure |
| Matches ordinary intuition ("I am not my body") | Brain damage and split-brain evidence tie mind to brain |

[warning]
The standard objection came from Princess Elisabeth of Bohemia in
1643: if the mind is non-spatial, what force could it exert, through
what mechanism? "The mind moves the body" names the mystery; it does
not solve it.
[/warning]

## 3. Physicalism: Mind as Brain

**Physicalism** (materialism) holds that everything is physical, so
every mental state is identical with, or realised by, a physical
state. The type-identity theory of Place and Smart claimed each
mental *type* is a brain *type*; **reductionism** translates mental
talk into neuroscience; **non-reductive** physicalism lets mental
explanations stand while depending on the physical.

| Variety | Claim | Shared liability |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Reductive identity | Pain *is* C-fibre firing (or its local equivalent) | Multiple realisability — aliens and AI may feel without our neurons |
| Eliminativism | Folk concepts like "belief" will go the way of phlogiston | Explains away what needed explaining |
| Non-reductive | The mental depends on, but is not reducible to, the physical | Risks epiphenomenalism — mind as a shadow |

Philosophers' sympathies show the state of play:

@chart donut title="PhilPapers 2020 survey: is the mind physical?" labels:
Physicalism
Non-physicalism
Other or undecided

data:
52
32
16
@/chart

[info]
A majority, but hardly a rout: a third of professional philosophers
reject physicalism — which is why the alternatives below remain live
options, not museum pieces.
[/info]

## 4. Behaviourism: Mind as Disposition

**Logical (analytical) behaviourism** — Ryle, Hempel — tried to
*dissolve* the problem: mental talk just *means* talk about
behaviour and dispositions to behave. To believe it will rain *is*
to be disposed to carry an umbrella; there is no ghostly inner item
to locate.

| Appeal | Fatal objection |
| --- | --- |
| No occult entities; fits the scientific method | No mental state can be defined purely in behaviour |
| Matches how we ascribe minds to others | Pretence, suppression, and a pain-reporting robot break the translation |

Hilary Putnam's **super-spartans** — people who feel pain but never
show it — drove the point home: behaviour without inner states is
conceivable, so inner states cannot be *defined* as behaviour.
Behaviourism survives today as a method (psychology may *study*
behaviour), not as a definition of the mental.

## 5. Functionalism: Mind as Software

**Functionalism** (Putnam, Lewis) defines mental states by their
**causal role**: what typically causes them, what they cause, and
how they relate to other states. Pain = the state typically caused
by tissue damage that causes withdrawal and distress — *whatever
substance plays that role*. Silicon, neurons, or alien biochemistry
are all eligible.

| Theory | Mind is like… | Key objection |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Dualism | A rider on a horse | The interaction problem |
| Behaviourism | The horse's visible gait | Inner life is not just behaviour |
| Identity theory | The horse's musculature | Multiple realisability |
| Functionalism | The gait *pattern*, portable across bodies | Inverted or absent qualia — same role, different feel? |

Functionalism is the working philosophy of cognitive science and AI:
if mind is organisation rather than substance, then a sufficiently
well-organised computer could, in principle, think.

## 6. The Chinese Room

John Searle (1980) imagined himself in a room, shuffling Chinese
symbols by rule books so clever that outsiders swear the room
understands Chinese. Searle's claim: he manipulates symbols
**without understanding a word** — syntax is not semantics.

| Stance | Reply |
| --- | --- |
| Systems reply | The *room as a whole*, not the man, understands |
| Robot reply | Ground the symbols in sensors and action; meaning follows |
| Searle's counter | Even the whole system still only pushes tokens |

[important]
The Chinese Room targets **strong AI** — the claim that a correctly
programmed machine literally *has* a mind — not the practical
success of AI. It is an argument about whether symbol manipulation
suffices for understanding, and it remains the reference point for
every "does the chatbot really mean it?" debate.
[/important]

## 7. Zombies and Qualia

A **philosophical zombie** (Chalmers) is a molecule-for-molecule
duplicate of you with no inner experience at all — it behaves,
speaks of pain, writes poems, and yet *nothing is like anything* for
it. If such a being is genuinely conceivable, Chalmers argues, then
experience is an extra fact over and above the physical — a lever
against physicalism.

**Qualia** are the felt qualities of experience: the redness of red,
the sting of chilli. Frank Jackson's **knowledge argument**: Mary,
the colour scientist raised in a black-and-white room, knows every
physical fact about colour — yet on first seeing red she seems to
learn something new. So, the argument concludes, physical facts do
not exhaust the mental.

| Objection to Mary | Reply |
| --- | --- |
| She gains an *ability*, not knowledge (ability hypothesis) | But knowing *what red is like* seems to be knowledge |
| She gains acquaintance, not new facts | Then what counts as "a fact" reopens the whole case |

## 8. The Hard Problem — and What AI Forces Us to Decide

David Chalmers (1995) split the science of mind in two. **Easy
problems** — attention, memory, behavioural report, self-modelling —
are hard engineering but tractable: explain the mechanisms and you
are done. The **hard problem** asks why any of that processing is
*accompanied by experience at all*.

| Position | Answer to the hard problem |
| --- | --- |
| Physicalism | Experience is physical; the apparent gap is conceptual |
| Illusionism (Frankish) | There is nothing to explain — the "phenomenal" is a user illusion |
| Panpsychism | Experience is a fundamental feature of matter itself |
| Dualism | Experience is non-physical; the hard problem is real |

AI sharpens the question from speculation into engineering. Large
language models pass behavioural tests no zombie could fail —
reporting, reasoning, pleading — yet whether there is "something it
is like" to be one is precisely what functionalism says yes to and
Searle says no to. Our theories of mind are no longer academic: they
govern how we treat machines, and how we treat each other when
neuroscience can read brains.

## 9. Quick Revision

| Concept | One-line recall |
| --- | --- |
| Mind–body problem | How does felt experience relate to physical brains? |
| Descartes | Two substances; killer objection: interaction |
| Behaviourism | Mind = dispositions to behave; dead as definition, alive as method |
| Functionalism | Mental states = causal roles; multiply realisable |
| Chinese Room | Syntax is not semantics — targets strong AI |
| Hard problem | Why is processing accompanied by experience at all? |
